    "summary": "Scorching Big River, Let It Grow>Deal and tight second‑set jams",
    "blurb": "The show shines with a blistering Big River, a soaring Let It Grow → Deal transition, and powerful second‑set explorations of The Other One and Wharf Rat. Jerry’s lead work is strong for most of the night, while Keith lifts Bertha with a standout organ solo.",
    "review": "May 10, 1978 at Veterans Memorial Coliseum captures the Dead in high‑octane form. The first set builds momentum from a tentative Jack Straw into a fire‑breathing Big River and a beautifully rendered Peggy‑O before launching into an electrifying Let It Grow → Deal sequence that sets the tone for the night. The second set, despite a sluggish Bertha, recovers spectacularly with a thunderous The Other One and an expansive Wharf Rat jam that showcases Phil’s “bomb” bass accents and Mickey’s extended drumming. Highlights also include tight performances of They Love Each Other, Me And My Uncle, It Must Have Been The Roses, Estimated Prophet → Eyes Of The World, and a bright Sugar Magnolia to close the set, followed by an upbeat U.S. Blues encore.\n\nAcross the three available sources, the show is consistently praised for its energy and improvisational peaks. While the soundboard capture (gd78-05-10.aud.fishman) suffers from tape hiss, it still delivers a usable mix that lets Jerry’s scorching solos on Big River and his vocal nuance on Peggy‑O shine through. The matrix source offers crystal‑clear balance for those who prefer pristine audio. Overall, the performance stands out as a solid 1978 outing with memorable moments that merit repeated listening.",
